A||||25||Seanh||Water in the chambers?||||5||
Z||000000||Seanh||10-20-2000||11:04 PM||||I was just wondering. Besides having a bad head gasket, how else could water get in the cumbustion chambers? I have white smoke comming from the tail pipes for about 6 months now and recently developed a coolant leak I am pretty sure is comming from the heads. Normally with my luck, I will get the heads all off, and it wont be my problem. I am about 95% sure this is the problem, but just wondering what else it could be.<P>------------------<BR>Sean Heberly<BR>Silver 86 GT 2.8<BR>Killer Stereo System||||reg||5
Z||000001||My7Fieros||10-20-2000||11:35 PM||||Cracked block maybe? (lets hope not)..either way I cant believe you've been driving that car for 6 months like that (or that the car has even been running)..thats not good.<p>[This message has been edited by My7Fieros (edited 10-20-2000).]||||reg||1
Z||000002||fiero56||10-20-2000||11:38 PM||||Do a leak down or compression test. That should help narrow it down somewhat.||||reg||1
Z||000003||Seanh||10-20-2000||11:52 PM||||Yep, about 6 months. Its been getting worse latly and finally it started leaking out. I hope not a craked block. I have been getting really bad gas milage, about 8-12 mpg. I havent really noticed very much coolant loss, so it much be a pretty small leak. I am auctually hoping it is the heads, as strange as that sounds. I have already began taking the engine apart, so Im a little late for a compression test right now. I am going to rebuild my car completly in Jan or Feb, so I was going to wait to do a compression test until then, to decide if the engine should be rebuilt. I am hoping this is is a bad head gasket. My coolant leak seems to be comming from on top of the A/C compressor, but its not the crossover pipe that usally rusts out. I am pretty sure heads are the only other thing that could be leaking up there. <P>------------------<BR>Sean Heberly<BR>Silver 86 GT 2.8<BR>Killer Stereo System||||reg||1
Z||000004||Indiana_resto_guy||10-21-2000||12:26 AM||||Seanh,<P>I can't believe what I just read!<BR>Are you saying that you were already tearing the engine down and were going to wait till Jan. or Feb. to do a compression check? I'm wondering how this is (or was) going to be accomplished!<BR>Since you've already taken the thing apart just take it into a machine shop, (heads and block) have it cleaned and get a valve job done (along with cam bearings, freeze plugs ect.). They will check for cracks before they start the work and will inform you of bad components.<BR>This is something that should have been done PRIOR to disassembly!||||reg||1
Z||000005||Seanh||10-21-2000||05:57 AM||||The reason I am waiting until Jan or Feb is because I dont have the time or the money to rebuild the car now, I dont know what good a compression check would do me when I cant do a thing about it if it is bad. There are alot of other reasons to me tearing the engine apart. I need to clean the intake manifolds, repaint them, replace plastic vacumm hoses, I have an oil leak on the front rocker arm covers, and need to replace my exhaust manifold gasket because the car sounds like a lawn mower/weed eater/ricer engine. I am alread planning on having the heads decked and revalved at a shop. I just re-read your post and misread somthing to begin with. I am right now only planning on replacing the head gaskets and the above jobs at this time. The engine will be re-assembled and the car will be driven until either we get snow here, or I am ready to start on the rebuilding. All I have done so far is remove the throttle body, upper and middle intake plenum, and fuel rail/injector assembly. I am only planning on going as far as the heads if I find the gasket is bad, not the block. If it is indeed the block, the car will be parked til I am ready to rebuild it.<P>P.S. Doesnt it sound like bad heads or a bad head gasket to you? Extremly bad gas milage. white smoke on start up if the car has been setting a few minuts, now a coolant leak from that general area and it is not any of the hoses, also somthing I havent mentioned before, when I rev the car while in park or N, when I let off the gas, the car makes a strange sound, kinda sounds like there isnt enough gas going in and it make a kinda poping noise, sorry I cant describe it any better, but it seems normal, but then again, none of my other car have done it.<BR>------------------<BR>Sean Heberly<BR>Silver 86 GT 2.8<BR>Killer Stereo System<p>[This message has been edited by Seanh (edited 10-21-2000).]||||reg||1
Z||000006||Indiana_resto_guy||10-21-2000||09:24 AM||||OK, here's some fuel for thought:<BR>1) A compression check WILL identify a blown head gasket, since you haven't removed the heads as yet you may still do this. Just make it able to turn over with the starter and insulate ALL other disconnected HOT wires (ie. alternator ect.) On the other hand a compression check is not normally totaly reliable at identifying cracks on a cold engine.<BR>2) Another way to check for a blown head gasket to water passages only (you can't do this now), is to observe the coolant flow through the system looking for bubbles (this lots of times makes the cooling system leak or blows radiators) and causes frequent boil overs at normal tempratures due to the increased pressure from the injected air.<BR>3) The naked eye 99.9% of the time CANNOT dectect bad areas or cracks in cylinder heads or cylinder walls and adjacent areas. (espically on dirty parts) once cleaned however they usually jump out at you!<BR>4) Suggested action:<BR>  A) Perform compression check.<BR>  B) Take appropriate action for the results.<BR> (you may only need to pull one side at this time, check your results with literature you have.) If cylinders side by side are weak <BR>chances are good for a bad gasket.<BR>  C) If you pull the heads anyway and don't at least get them checked, then reassemble the engine as is, nothing will be accomplished and the problem will still persist. Therefore, time and money WASTED!<BR>  D) Say you pull the heads, at least get them checked for cracks (gaskets cost '$' too!) who knows, this may take care of all your top end problems if you go ahead and get the valve job at this time!||||reg||1
Z||000007||Seanh||10-21-2000||09:49 AM||||Okay, Ill do a compression check today. I have a question about doing it though. I have removed the distributor cap, and the plug wires. The spark plugs are still in the engine. My question, should I remove all the plugs to do the compression check, or remove them one at a time and replace them once finished? Thanks<P>------------------<BR>Sean Heberly<BR>Silver 86 GT 2.8<BR>Killer Stereo System||||reg||1
Z||000008||fierosound||10-21-2000||11:13 AM||||There are coolant lines running to the throttle body.  Coolant may be leaking past the throttle body/intake gasket into the intake.  Just replace the throttle body gasket if that is the problem.||||reg||10
Z||000009||StuGood||10-21-2000||12:06 PM||||fierosound, that occurred to me, too!  Great minds, and all that  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/wink.gif">.<P>Seanh, pull all the plugs to do the compression check.  Saves your starter + battery.  ||||reg||10
Z||000010||Seanh||10-21-2000||12:24 PM||||Alright, I got me a compression guage from Advanced Auto Parts and I will go out a do it here pretty soon. Ill post results once I get done. If my compression check yields nothing, I will finish my other jobs I have been wanting to do and reassemble the engine and replace all the gaskets. Thanks<P>------------------<BR>Sean Heberly<BR>Silver 86 GT 2.8<BR>Killer Stereo System||||reg||1
Z||000011||Seanh||10-21-2000||08:30 PM||||Okay, I got home and pulled all the plugs, cleaned up my parts making sure nothing was in the engine. Put my batt. back in and tried to crank it over. I lost all power to the car, dome lights and all, went back to check the battery, found nothing wrong, went back to the car and opened the door and the lights came on. I tired cranking it over again and once again lost all power for a few minutes. I slapped a 1.5A charger on the batt. and it said it was fully charged in less then 30 minutes. If the battery was drained to the point of where the whole car looses power, then a 1.5A charger would not have fully charged it in 30 minutes. I have removed quite a few sensors, maybe one that will not let the engine crank if it is bad/unpluged. Perhaps the TPS? The compression guage instructions say to remove all plugs and lock the throttle in the complete open position. Maybe I should plug the TPS in and hold it all the way open? Also, the wires going into the Distributor are unpluged as well as the fuel injector plugs.<P>------------------<BR>Sean Heberly<BR>Silver 86 GT 2.8<BR>Killer Stereo System||||reg||1
Z||000012||Seanh||10-21-2000||11:29 PM||||Ok guys. I got to thinking. I was originally planning on removing the heads, taking them in to be resurfaced and a valve job and cleaning and new seals done on them. They would inspect for cracks and whatnot. Once I have the heads out, I can identify a bad gasket, if this is indeed my problem right? Should I just forget doing the compression check now and continue the procedure for removing the heads? Its not really much more work from where I am now, and I am willing to do it. I havent ever had a boil-over yet. The car seems to leak coolant with or without any pressure in the system. It really gets to pouring out when there is pressure, but it will drip without pressure. Should I maybe fill the coolant and watch for drips? It would still work at this point. The reason I am thinking it is probally the gasket is because all the things tht have been happening. Car is getting bad milage and white smoke comming from the pipes on start up, slow acceleration and poor performance, now a bad leak comming from the front of the engine and it is not any hoses or crossover pipe. Right before I sprang the leak I was racing some guy a I know from a stop light and hard acceleration up to 70. I then backed off after beating him and we stopped and talked. I took off again and less then a half mile down the road I notice the car overheating, stop to check it out and see the puddle of coolant under the car. I pulled it home. Havent driven it since. Doenst it sound like a head gasket or cracked heads to you? What should I do? Thanks so much, keep it comming.<P>------------------<BR>Sean Heberly<BR>Silver 86 GT 2.8<BR>Killer Stereo System||||reg||1
Z||000013||fiero56||10-21-2000||11:52 PM||||There are so many variables, it is hard to properly diagnose this thing without a base line.<P>Bad mileage could be caused by one(or several)of any number of things, a number of sensors could be bad, vacuum leak, clogged cat, clogged fuel filter, bad fuel pump, etc. Overheating could be caused by: bad radiator cap, bad thermostat, bad heater core, crushed coolant pipe, bad coolant hose, etc. Poor acceleration could be bad ignition system or components, plus everything in the bad fuel mileage catergory.<P>A compression test will tell you how the top end is faring. If one(or several)cylinders show dramatically lower psi, you know something is wrong. <B>Usually</B> a bad head gasket is very easy to distinguish. A bad head is much harder to see with the eye. If one or more cylinders are dramatically lower, place a few drops of oil in the cylinder and retest. If it rises, rings are likely bad, if not, valves, or gasket are most likely the culprit.<P>White smoke when an engine is cold and first started is normal. If the vapor doesn't leave after several minutes, it is a sign something isn't right. Check the oil for coolant too.<P>Figure I should add: I find it difficult to believe coolant is finding its way from a water jacket all the way to the outside of the head is able to leak down the outside of the block. Seems to me, if a gasket ever got that bad, the compression from at least one cylinder would be able to find its way out too(coolant=16psi, cylinder=140+psi).<P><p>[This message has been edited by fiero56 (edited 10-22-2000).]||||reg||1
Z||000014||Seanh||10-22-2000||12:49 AM||||There are a few things I can eliminate to help maybe. I just replaced a MAP sensor when I got a code indicating a bad one. I havent driven the since, so I cant tell much. Yhere are no other codes present in the daily driving though. I have checked and found no vacuum leaks, but I did brake a plastic hose in the process, I am replacing with rodney kit. I also have a new cat, thought that was my problem, but no. Replaced fuel filter Jun of 99. Replaced fuel pump Jan of 99. I dont experience overheating at all until I got this leak. Radiator caps have been replaced, thermo hasnt been, but I am sure its good, heater core I havent touched, but seems fine, no crushed coolant pipes, all hoses are good, I check them all before deciding it was my heads. I have replaced distibutor, cap, rotor, plugs and wires last year. I have never had a boil-over since I owned the car. My car is not starting as quick as it was when I got the car. I could only get about 180 miles on a tank, now getting about 90 miles on a tank. The white smoke is just there for less then a minut. Just a poof when I start it up, but it even happens when the car is hot. Sorry I just kicked out most of the things you said, but I am just trying to narrow down my problem. Once again I might add, my car has never overheated until I developed the coolant leak, then it shot straight up and began leaking, probally because the pump got air-locked.||||reg||1
Z||000015||Seanh||10-22-2000||12:57 AM||||If white smoke is normal on start up like mine does, its quite possible that there is not coolant getting into the chambers at all then. Any ides on the origin of my leak then. I think my gasket for the TB is good.<P>------------------<BR>Sean Heberly<BR>Silver 86 GT 2.8<BR>Killer Stereo System||||reg||1
Z||000016||fiero56||10-22-2000||01:35 AM||||8mpg is horrible. I don't know what to think. What tranny is in there? Auto? Only sensors that come to mind that would affect it that terribly are oxygen sensor and water temp sensor that sends signal to ECM(not temp guage sendor). Possible major leakage with fuel injectors? Maybe fuel guage is lying? Maybe engine is <B>really</B> tired(I can't see one running very well if it were that bad though)? I would question the fuel guage, maybe overheating is from air in coolant system. Maybe cooant is escaping a block or drain plug? I am running low on ideas, most are pretty far stretches.||||reg||1
Z||000017||Seanh||10-22-2000||08:28 AM||||Yes, It is an auto. Water temp sensor has been replaced Jun of 99. Never touched the O2 sensor, but I figured I would be getting a code if it were bad, right? I am not sure on the fuel injectors. I never smell gas, nor did I see any signs of leakage when I just removed them. Gas guage is screwed up, but I never rely on it, I fill up when I hit 100M on the meter, and it will take about 9 gallons. Tried fuel injector cleaner, nothing. Iam almost sure the overheating is from the coolant not circulating, because air in the system, but I have <B>never</B> had that car overheat since I got it, first time it did I checked and found it was leaking. Other then that the car runs fine, idles perfect, a little erratic, but fine. It doesnt have the acceleration it had when I first got it though, nor the gas milage. Still, when I first got it, I only got 175 or so miles on a tank. I would die to get that now. I do have a lead foot, and I recently found out how much that can affect gas milage in one of my other cars. When Im in the Fiero I have a little trouble holding myself back from flooring it alot.  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif"><P>------------------<BR>Sean Heberly<BR>Silver 86 GT 2.8<BR>Killer Stereo System<p>[This message has been edited by Seanh (edited 10-22-2000).]||||reg||1
Z||000018||Indiana_resto_guy||10-22-2000||10:05 AM||||It's beginning to sound like a trashed water pump and worn out timing chain and gears.<BR>A bad water pump front seal is hard to see in the Fiero's engine compartment 4's and 6's alike. Since you did some heavy accecleration and rapid decelleration (did you down shift too?) the old chain could have been streched enough to toss the timing out enough to cause the over heating condition and poor perfomance and BAD fuel mileage.<BR>Pop the front cover and see what you find.||||reg||3
Z||000019||fiero56||10-22-2000||11:29 AM||||I can't type long, have to leave in a minute. If the crank(or cam)jumped a tooth on the timing chain, the symptoms are possible, plus with heavy acceleration/deceleration, and high miles, it is possible. I would check the water pump seal.||||reg||1
Z||000020||Seanh||10-22-2000||11:39 AM||||That day that I first got the leak was the day I raced, but it is an auto, and no, I didnt down shift it. I went out a bought a waterpump gasket because I thought that was my inital problem. My gas milage has been this bad for a while, about 7 months. Are you refering to poping off the rocker arm/valve cover, or the timing chain cover? I will have a better look at it right now, but I still, since I am already this far, remove and rebuild the heads and valves.<P>------------------<BR>Sean Heberly<BR>Silver 86 GT 2.8<BR>Killer Stereo System||||reg||1
Z||000021||Seanh||10-22-2000||12:37 PM||||Good god, I must be the dumbest fiero owner that ever lived, I not longer think I am qualified to work on my car. <B>I found my stupid leak!</B> I dont know rather I am happy or pissed though. It is not in the heads or head gaskets though, the reason I am pissed. It is comming from a tiny hole in a metal pipe, let me elaborate. I decided, since my leak happens even where the car is not running, to fill the thermo housing thing with water and see what happens, I did so, and watched, and nothing. I then decided to fill the large hose comming into the thermo housing (the thermo housing is removed), so I filled it to the point of where the hole in the engine started pouring water, watched and nothing. I thought to my self, all these things that test for leaks do it apply pressure to the system, so I got some cleaner, cleaned the end of the hose and blew into it. I noticed that water was starting to come up thru the thermo housing hole, and saw no other leaks. I then found a little hose, the one that goes in the thermo housing right under the large one connecting the crossover pipe to the housing, and blew in it, low and behold, my leak appered. But, I thought this strange, the leak did not come from the hose I was blowing into, it came from another hose. You know that thing comming off the A/C compressor, looks kinda cilander like, connects to the compressor on the side, and to an a/c hose on the bottem, okay? Then the a/c hose it hooks to veers sharply down and to the drivers side of the car, right were that a/c hose veers down, there is a hose touching it, they are kinda mashed together like they are supposed to be different hoses, but there wanst enough room or somthing??? There is a little gap between them and when I blew into that other hose on the thermo housing, I see a little line of coolant shooting from the hose right were it make contact (or is supposed to) with the a/c hose. it sprays on the round thing off the a/c compressor and drips on to the exhaust pipe after the cat converter. Any one still following me after that? I am really bad at explaining things like this, but I hope some one gets the idea of what I am talking about. Anyone have a part number or any information I can go to the parts store with? Thanks so much.<P>------------------<BR>Sean Heberly<BR>Silver 86 GT 2.8<BR>Killer Stereo System||||reg||1
Z||000022||StuGood||10-22-2000||02:07 PM||||Be glad.  You are no longer the dumbest Fiero owner that ever lived  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/wink.gif"><P>Not sure, but I think the hose you described could be a heater hose - and tell them you want the one that's in <B>back</B>, in the engine compartment.  Pull it off the car and take it with you to make sure you get the proper replacement.  Possible that generic heater hose could be cut to length and used.<BR>Good luck  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif"> ||||reg||14
Z||000023||Seanh||10-22-2000||02:40 PM||||I dont have a vehical large enough to take the pipe in town, and it would look stange seeing it tied ontop of a car or somthing. It is a metal pipe, not a rubber hose, I guess I kept useing hose in my last posted, but no, it is a metal pipe running from the back to front, runs on the pass side of the gas tank, covered with insulation, smaller of the two running up on the pass side. It has been decided that at this time I am going to continue to remove the heads, since I need to replace the rocker arm cover gasket anyway, and take them in to be resurfaced and 5 angle valve job performed, as well as inspection for cracks or bad compontents. Anything else I could and should do while Im down here?<P>------------------<BR>Sean Heberly<BR>Silver 86 GT 2.8<BR>Killer Stereo System||||reg||1
